<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wahunsenacah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1547–1618</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Chief Powhatan, Wahunsonacock</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wahunsenacah, known to the English as Powhatan, was the paramount chief of the Powhatan Confederacy in Tidewater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>. Inheriting six chiefdoms, he extended his rule over some 30 Algonquian-speaking peoples, governing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/werowocomoco">Werowocomoco</a>. He dealt with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a> colonists from 1607 by turns through trade and war, and his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pocahontas">Pocahontas</a>'s marriage in 1614 brought peace until his death in 1618.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wahunsenacah.jpg" alt="Wahunsenacah" width="320" height="460" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: engraved by William Hole · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Powhatan_john_smith_map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
